-- title:
--   Prolongation residues are compatible along the degeneracy tower
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`, a level $N \ge 1$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$ and a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red} \colon A \to k$. At level $N$ one is given `data : ModularPolynomialData q`, i.e. a monic $\Phi \in \mathbb Z[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(q)$ annihilating the pair $(j, j_q)$ of $q$-expansions, together with `hKr`, the Kronecker congruence $\Phi \equiv (X^q - Y)(X - Y^q) \bmod q$, and hypotheses `hα`, `hβ` asserting that the two degeneracy inclusions $\overline{\mathbb Q}\!\cdot\!F_N \to \overline{\mathbb Q}\!\cdot\!F_{Nq}$ of base-changed full modular function fields inside $\overline{\mathbb Q}((q))$ are integral ring maps; $P$ is a `PlaceSpecialization` for these data and $R$ a `ProlongationTuple` over $P$, which in particular provides two regular prolongations $R_1, R_2$ of $A$ to `modularFunctionFieldBar (N * q)` and residue maps `residue₁`, `residue₂` with values in `modularFunctionFieldC k N`, the subfield of $k((q))$ generated by the reductions of $j$ and $j_N$. Let $\ell \ge 1$ with $q \nmid N\ell$, and let $P_r$, $R_r$ be a second, unrelated place specialisation and prolongation tuple for the same $A$, $k$, $\mathrm{red}$ at level $N\ell$, with its own `dataᵣ`, `hKrᵣ`, `hαᵣ`, `hβᵣ`. Then for every $f$ in `modularFunctionFieldBar (N * q)` and for each of the two indices $i \in \{1,2\}$: if $f$ lies in the valuation subring $(R.R_i).\mathrm{integers}$, then its image under the tower inclusion `towerInclBar` associated with $Nq \mid N\ell q$ lies in $(R_r.R_i).\mathrm{integers}$, and the $i$-th residue of that image, regarded as an element of $k((q))$ via `modularFunctionFieldC k (N * ℓ)`, equals the $i$-th residue of $f$, regarded in $k((q))$ via `modularFunctionFieldC k N`.
--
--   This is the statement that the two prolongations of $A$ to the modular function field at level $Nq$ restrict from those at level $N\ell q$ along the forgetful (first degeneracy) inclusion, with unchanged residual $q$-expansions, on each of the two components of the special fibre; informally, the restriction of a Gauss prolongation is the Gauss prolongation. It is used in the computation of the $y$-depth of a function restricted along `towerInclBar` in terms of the ramification index along the mod-$q$ Hecke $\alpha$-map.
